WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:04.604
NARRATOR: Like Yasuhiro Kubo
here, going for a Guinness

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:05.638
world record title.

00:00:08.541 --> 00:00:11.611
He'll be free falling
from around 10,000 feet

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:13.947
and attempting to catch
up with his parachute

00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:16.750
attached to this canister.

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:21.287
The record is determined by how
long he waits before jumping.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:25.759
At 50 seconds
later, off he goes.

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:28.194
[music playing]

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:44.711
Well, that was a relief--

00:00:44.778 --> 00:00:45.678
and a world record.

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.085
Free falling without a
parachute is one of the most

00:00:52.152 --> 00:00:54.454
dangerous stunts imaginable.

00:00:54.521 --> 00:01:00.093
Do not even consider considering
to consider to do it, ever.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.929
Especially when even regular
skydivers have their off days.

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:09.335
A little help please, bro?

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:10.970
Go on, you can do it.

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:12.205
Go on.

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:15.542
Great, thank you.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:18.545
All right, so how does a
skydiver fall fast enough

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.079
to catch up with
a parachute thrown

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:22.115
out nearly a minute beforehand?

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:25.218
Well, to find out, we need
to brush up on terminal

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:29.589
velocity and air resistance.

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:31.991
As an object falls, it
collides with trillions

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:36.896
of tiny air molecules,
resulting in air resistance.

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:39.933
As the object accelerates, the
air resistance acting on it

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:45.004
increases until it matches the
force of the object's weight.

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:48.074
It's now at terminal
velocity, the maximum speed

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:50.443
it could fall.

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.446
A larger surface area
increases air resistance,

00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:56.850
and so decreases
terminal velocity.

00:01:56.916 --> 00:02:00.119
A smaller surface area
decreases air resistance,

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:02.489
and so increases
terminal velocity.

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:07.527
A skydiver in spread
eagle position

00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:10.697
hits terminal velocity
around 120 miles an hour

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.499
after about 12 seconds.

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:14.901
But for Yasuhiro to
catch up with his chute,

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:16.536
that is just too slow.

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:19.038
So which of our
wannabe record breakers

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:22.141
has remembered how we speed
up our terminal velocity?

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:26.112
Not these ones.

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:29.749
That is the complete opposite.

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.918
Their raft has a
large surface area,

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:33.887
thereby increasing
air resistance

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:35.021
and slowing them down.

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.828
OK, anyone else?

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.065
Yeah, that's it.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:48.568
Going upside down and
reducing his surface area

00:02:48.635 --> 00:02:53.373
decreases air resistance and
increases terminal velocity.

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:58.478
Trouble is-- oh, whoa,
it's very hard to control.

00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:02.749
Oh, that guy upside down, too?

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:04.751
Yes, he was.

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.185
Once he's caught
up with his chute,

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:09.989
Yasuhiro needed to steer himself
into position to grab it.

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:11.391
How did he do that?

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:14.294
Well, skydivers can also use
air resistance to maneuver.

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:20.833
For example, by
adjusting his body shape,

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.503
this guy deflects flex
more air backwards,

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:27.106
which pushes him forwards.

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:29.108
Bullseye?

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:32.211
Somehow, all of our
high flyers were fine,

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:33.279
but I think we should leave
the record to Yasuhiro.

